--TEST--
`Core\IO\File::lock`'s bound is one file and it is named on both sides — three handles on three files hold three locks at once, while a second spelling of a path already held is refused, and a `close` frees the file it named and no other — spec § 14
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The cases already on disk all ask about one file, so each of them passes
// unchanged against an implementation whose lock was a single flag on the
// program: one handle takes it, the next is refused, `close` puts it back.
// What that implementation cannot do is the line below, and what tells the
// two apart is naming the bound from *outside* as well as from inside.
Core\IO\File $a = Core\IO::open("lock-a.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::ReadWrite);
Core\IO\File $b = Core\IO::open("lock-b.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::ReadWrite);
Core\IO\File $c = Core\IO::open("lock-c.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::ReadWrite);
$a->lock();
$b->lock();
$c->lock();
echo "three files, three locks", "\n";

// The other side of the same bound, so that the three grants above are a
// statement about files rather than a member that stopped refusing.
Core\IO\File $again = Core\IO::open("lock-a.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::ReadWrite);
try {
    $again->lock();
    echo "one file, two locks", "\n";
} catch (IOError $busy) {
    echo "same file: refused", "\n";
}

// And the file is the file, not the string that reached it. A lock keyed on
// the path as spelled would hand this one out, and every case in this tree
// would still be green — the refusal comes from the operating system, which
// was never looking at the argument.
Core\IO\File $spelled = Core\IO::open("./lock-b.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::ReadWrite);
try {
    $spelled->lock();
    echo "a second spelling won its own lock", "\n";
} catch (IOError $busy) {
    echo "another spelling: refused", "\n";
}

// Releasing is bounded the same way. `close` frees the file that handle
// named; the two it did not name are still held, which is the half a
// single-file case has no second file to ask about.
$b->close();
$spelled->lock();
echo "freed, the other spelling takes it", "\n";
try {
    $again->lock();
    echo "the close freed a file it did not name", "\n";
} catch (IOError $stillA) {
    echo "a: still held", "\n";
}
Core\IO\File $onC = Core\IO::open("lock-c.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::ReadWrite);
try {
    $onC->lock();
    echo "c went free as well", "\n";
} catch (IOError $stillC) {
    echo "c: still held", "\n";
}

$a->close();
$c->close();
$again->close();
$spelled->close();
$onC->close();
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
three files, three locks
same file: refused
another spelling: refused
freed, the other spelling takes it
a: still held
c: still held
